cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Offline Bangla Disaster-Response Question Answering with Compact Open-Source
  LLMs: A CPU-Only Evaluation of Accuracy, Safety, and Efficiency Trade-offs
message: >-
  If you use this benchmark, adversarial probe, or the refuse-by-default
  wrapper, please cite this paper and reuse the released item ids so that
  results remain comparable across studies.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: TODO
    given-names: TODO
repository-code: TODO
url: TODO
abstract: >-
  A CPU-only, fully offline evaluation of six compact open-weight language
  models on Bangla disaster-response question answering, measuring accuracy,
  safety (over-answering on unanswerable, false-premise, out-of-scope, and
  unknowable items), and efficiency on the same items in the same run. The
  study releases a 634-item human-verified benchmark with per-item safety
  labels, a 64-item adversarial answerability probe, a strict human-calibrated
  cross-encoder scoring protocol, and a deterministic refuse-by-default
  wrapper. Best strict accuracy is 25.7 percent; all six models over-answer on
  the majority of adversarial traps, and the wrapper reduces over-answering to
  0.0-6.2 percent.
keywords:
  - Bangla
  - natural language processing
  - question answering
  - disaster response
  - large language models
  - quantization
  - AI safety
  - abstention
  - inter-annotator agreement
